                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1543-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ><img id="1543-1" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_c.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Cardioid" title="Cardioid" ><img id="1543-2" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_8.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Bidirectional" title="Bidirectional" ></span>Apex Electronics 580</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Multi-Pattern Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>The Apex 580 is a multipattern large-diaphragm condenser. It appears to be identical to the Alctron BC400; see link in sidebar.</p><p>The mic&#8217;s capsule is described as a 35mm model, and is almost certainly a K67 type. It is a dual-diaphragm model, and provides multiple polar patterns.</p><p>Three small switches on the front of the microphone body allow the following configuration options:</p><ul><li>Polar pattern: Omni, Cardioid, Figure-8.</li><li>Attenuation pad: 0dB (flat), -10dB, -15dB.</li><li>High-pass filter: 0dB (flat), -6dB @ 75Hz, -6dB @ 150Hz.</li></ul><p>The mic&#8217;s published self-noise rating, 11dBA, is excellent.</p><p>Although Apex has not specified the nature of the mic&#8217;s output circuit, we presume it is transformerless, based on the mic&#8217;s similarity to the Alctron BC400.</p><p>According to the Apex website, the mic ships with an aluminum flightcase and a &#8220;widescreen.&#8221; We assume that means a foam shield, not a television. The microphone probably also includes a shockmount, as it is pictured as such by Apex dealers.<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/Apex-Electronics/580">Apex Electronics 580</a> is also known as: Apex580.</p>
